#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171761 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "2a7b5d23-3dc7-57f8-98ed-72b1a45e787a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8157" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/c3a4052f80fc4a7202d410f3ea523e58507ac60399506c2abad9be164bce5251.png)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/18abb9c5165a3fe54a0f3067cf5325bfd4bd0fa4243c841ce4e7bb95c25dddda.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171762 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7d2dc7ea-947d-5cda-8c95-c28281ecd792" :question/math-academy-id "e-8158" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/c344041bbcf1cc644f0cfb1e5a3ac8ecd687db483425e4f1f4bb19c76ebac439.png)\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D=P^T \\! A P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=5\\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/7b0367066950407149521e6b06255f71b25fe5e67bc609fac5e914d3d1315fe7.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/498ecfed1cfe5ed4b1df8c02088045e67c4d5601e47f403288d271447c5ded28.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/0aa3aecabd941ccc8e176aef479eb3630197ff9c7fb6ca63e028e41315c9aafb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/1f494272dcd6828d437e2f566e46aa5b5ece49a92cd82af094e25834fe1bbcbe.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/737d19f3df39ef56518c98843cb7dd0359965570892b17df57fb4117bb0f5844.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171763 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "fd780d94-dd75-5a57-b34d-4bbaffcb95ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8164" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/9614ff63a66cf5428a4107828b08ba16c43e3e021f6620d408d8b70358a3e535.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/9acec021ce321e77ad541ecbb0a38944aa4991ec26c721e0e59a521490269d06.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/efb691aec933aa631974add9dc9d7d122b03f5956b17459eb0e76751e07ea42a.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/8c0ac2201e3e40bcc8e5e71c12413504694b201237069a4695fae94d33e7fcb4.png)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/1490159efc1ac2073ee029f918c136b7c16fd77b5f15e59334a3ae63e08a8c92.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b188ff77-03ff-5510-b5e2-bca817cb4404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381732} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d9b6b8-d0ae-5f1c-8379-ae8b9541e94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381733} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ff82f-4274-5773-bfff-eb57d39333ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381734} {:answer/id #uuid "4da0b285-d891-54ee-974d-32910d2fbcb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381735} {:answer/id #uuid "84f64a81-9734-56f9-94de-2b1ec0e18288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f64a81-9734-56f9-94de-2b1ec0e18288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381736} :answer-field/id #uuid "87af28df-0eb0-5393-83fc-f9f885dbc8e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d846b7d3-a0cb-5fe4-88c4-a04c109b53b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140780" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76d4b87b-989a-542b-b045-176902fedd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381739} {:answer/id #uuid "39e54b22-2e2b-590c-adb6-a7fbc18c2131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381740} {:answer/id #uuid "97ec03b2-c163-59d5-9bcd-84591551f85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381741} {:answer/id #uuid "82618c0e-0826-5ff2-ba01-4a2eb5cf7832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381742} {:answer/id #uuid "4e447fd3-4ab1-58ba-ad7f-5c31eab23ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76d4b87b-989a-542b-b045-176902fedd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381739} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd1dcf4-3f09-58bb-a9b3-b7454b8d744d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e21a37fd-af47-56e6-bbdf-7f961f5a8890" :question/math-academy-id "q-140825" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "291b11be-79ac-53d5-8527-6e62732a3102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381746} {:answer/id #uuid "c34ed81e-7246-5047-b5f8-f44136b27b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381747} {:answer/id #uuid "237b57d9-d4a4-5bb5-9de5-c918bc4f4383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381748} {:answer/id #uuid "9babe688-f5ac-5e2e-82bd-3bb6099e5083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381749} {:answer/id #uuid "23fc848c-4225-59fb-9991-919fecb4cd69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9babe688-f5ac-5e2e-82bd-3bb6099e5083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381749} :answer-field/id #uuid "967da6d5-b2c0-5475-a894-15ee082b7114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07ca1eab-cf66-5ca0-a68c-c42b61de1f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-140828" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c249f0ca-0689-57ba-be14-78b29d58eb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381753} {:answer/id #uuid "29505878-d51c-5903-b65b-1366e184a35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381754} {:answer/id #uuid "8bbbe46d-6b4c-5be1-a491-e2fdf20f1ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381755} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a810b0-fe6b-592f-838d-a7a89164c492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381756} {:answer/id #uuid "3801148c-2632-5e3f-9816-86880987a32b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1a810b0-fe6b-592f-838d-a7a89164c492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381756} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b759d66-2e46-5056-9d8d-d0c242220009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2aafa1a-4171-5fdd-a862-1ddb2e8d35de" :question/math-academy-id "q-93999" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1328227b-61e5-5eac-9edd-c9075e1c803f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381760} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6a3f3c-1263-5a40-b812-670bfb8a4679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381761} {:answer/id #uuid "464df55a-8264-5d24-a645-d4471583fa5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{align