begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb700f59-18b6-5a41-9fe3-6393db88e7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381781} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba9fd1-2035-55ef-aa7c-4c4cf56f7bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381782} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5dc606-86fb-5767-b47e-fdcc5bb061d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381783} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6383a7-d3db-5fd2-a40d-3390587cf190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381784} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2ab260-e294-5bfc-b2bf-0e264cfaddfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb700f59-18b6-5a41-9fe3-6393db88e7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381781} :answer-field/id #uuid "811611eb-fe64-5aa2-a626-d93b022ae577" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e266e00f-9fa3-5a93-849a-c0c7dd5a8f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-94032" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c0bd11-e730-59f8-8fb8-deaa441bed5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381788} {:answer/id #uuid "d952aeea-010f-55cb-afb4-8c7cd7c0ac31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381789} {:answer/id #uuid "e26bf280-17b0-5c1d-ad33-cd87a9ec0727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381790} {:answer/id #uuid "f6640f86-119d-5f16-a314-99249b431084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381791} {:answer/id #uuid "517fe7b0-ede9-5cc2-bba3-ab51f073c91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44c0bd11-e730-59f8-8fb8-deaa441bed5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381788} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a631074-7259-582f-921d-21de94eb740e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01d6bc91-3005-5cb6-9f58-ca82d4ab3b4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94035" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c2e06c-de78-5ace-bd8e-cf5e89a9fdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381795} {:answer/id #uuid "b35c4888-dee7-5c0e-9bee-e9a92b738cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381796} {:answer/id #uuid "c83d7e4f-a53d-59b0-b8be-23ea53594dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381797} {:answer/id #uuid "fbee763b-f98d-58a3-ab19-124054876ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381798} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cc78ff-2779-597d-8ab8-3011092b2c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47c2e06c-de78-5ace-bd8e-cf5e89a9fdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381795} :answer-field/id #uuid "af36f979-d3e9-5bee-9f77-1fe325401ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5923242-1a47-5487-a3a8-4c37dcb336a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94037" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ Computing $A-(-9)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+9I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e18c3aca-cd91-5982-be76-dbc3595cedf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381802} {:answer/id #uuid "6933fe82-6ff3-5eab-9c92-a47254138f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381803} {:answer/id #uuid "981b9790-cf11-552c-895e-8756a9c1c1eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381804} {:answer/id #uuid "de7d1cbe-0cde-5a3d-b5da-4a11cdf9d16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381805} {:answer/id #uuid "6c77a9be-395a-52e9-a008-f35adc1bbcc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de7d1cbe-0cde-5a3d-b5da-4a11cdf9d16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381805} :answer-field/id #uuid "28ca353c-33ec-5e0a-8a89-7638677ccf11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45313e55-9dc2-5f9b-a30e-26241f08ab4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94039" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Computing $A-(-5)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b005adc1-e9e1-5b7e-a7fd-e3dc66f89bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & -\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381809} {:answer/id #uuid "702ec27f-7777-501a-a7ac-cb418ba3c7f4" :a