 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7ed7ae-7d73-5592-ade5-3c5343129de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381816} {:answer/id #uuid "64163ae4-9aa8-5e3e-a671-ca4791a0a755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381817} {:answer/id #uuid "be09f5c7-ba3a-5d07-baf7-748c9825e24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381818} {:answer/id #uuid "613248f3-be53-5ec7-8a5b-c89309c35cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381819} {:answer/id #uuid "bb44e8b5-37ab-5621-a59a-118ca14dfe23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and 